from helpers import *

def part4b(D):
    h = []
    h.append('<h2 id="steel">4.6 Steel basics and identifying salvaged steel</h2>')
    h.append('<p>The single most important number about steel is its <b>carbon content</b>. Below about 0.3% carbon (mild steel, rebar, bolts, angle iron, nails) steel cannot be meaningfully hardened by quenching: it is easy to forge and weld and good for hooks, hinges, brackets and tongs. Above about 0.5–0.6% (springs, files, blades, tool steels) steel becomes very hard when heated and quenched: it is needed for anything that cuts or is struck — knives, chisels, punches, axe bits. <b>Cast iron</b> (2–4% carbon: engine blocks, old pans, brake drums, pipe fittings, stove parts) cannot be forged at all: it crumbles under the hammer.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Salvage item", "Probable steel", "Carbon", "Quench", "Good for"], [
        ["Vehicle leaf springs", "5160 / similar (Cr-alloy spring steel)", "~0.6%", "Oil", "Knives, machetes, chisels, punches, drifts, hardy tools, froes"],
        ["Coil springs", "5160, 9260 or similar", "~0.6%", "Oil", "Punches, chisels, small tools, tong reins (annealed)"],
        ["Old files and rasps", "Plain high-carbon (1095/W1-type); a few cheap files are case-hardened mild steel", "~1.0–1.25%", "Water/brine (risk of cracking) or warm oil", "Knives, scrapers, small chisels, fire steels. Anneal before forging"],
        ["Disc harrow blades, plow discs", "1080-type or boron steel", "~0.8%", "Oil or water", "Machetes, large knives, hoe and spade blades"],
        ["Circular and bandsaw blades (non-carbide)", "1075 / 15N20 / L6-type", "~0.75%", "Oil", "Thin knives, scrapers, saws"],
        ["Ball and roller bearings", "52100", "~1.0%", "Oil", "Small blades, punches (needs careful heat control)"],
        ["Axle and drive shafts, forklift tines", "Medium-carbon alloy (1040–4140-type)", "0.4%", "Oil", "Hammers, drifts, punches, hot cuts, anvils"],
        ["Jackhammer bits, rock drill steel", "Shock-resisting tool steel", "~0.5%", "Oil/air (varies)", "Hot and cold chisels, punches, hardies"],
        ["Railroad rail", "Rail steel", "~0.7–0.8%", "—", "Anvils; head can be forged into hammers, hardy tools"],
        ["Railroad spikes", "Low carbon (“HC” stamp ≈ 0.3%)", "0.15–0.3%", "—", "Hooks, small tools, handles — not good edges"],
        ["Rebar", "Unknown, variable scrap steel", "0.3–0.5%", "Test it", "Tongs reins, hooks, grates, fire tools, rough tools. Avoid coated (epoxy/galvanized) rebar"],
        ["Bolts, angle iron, sheet, bed frames, structural", "Mild steel (some bed-frame angle is high-carbon rail steel — it cracks when bent cold)", "&lt;0.3%", "—", "Hinges, brackets, hooks, nails, tongs, stands"],
        ["Wrought iron (old wagon tires, chain, fences pre-1900)", "Nearly pure iron with slag fibers", "&lt;0.1%", "—", "Forge-welds superbly; shows wood-grain texture when etched or broken"]]))
    h.append(fig(D.spark_test(), "<b>Figure 8. Spark test.</b> More carbon = more bursts (“stars”) and forks. Alloying elements change the color and shorten the stream (based on the spark characteristics described in U.S. Army TC 9-524)."))
    h.append('<p><b>Other quick tests:</b> (1) <b>Magnet</b> — all steels and iron are magnetic; most stainless (300-series) and non-ferrous metals are not. (2) <b>File test</b> — a file skates on hardened steel and bites into mild steel or annealed steel. (3) <b>Quench test</b> — heat the end of a sample to just past non-magnetic, quench in water, then try to file it and snap it in a vise: high-carbon steel becomes glass-hard and snaps with a fine gray grain; mild steel bends. (4) <b>Break</b> — a fine, velvety gray fracture means hardened high-carbon steel; coarse sparkling crystals mean overheated or low-quality steel. Label every piece of tested steel with a paint mark — mixed-up scrap is the most common beginner problem.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="heat">4.7 Heating colors</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.heat_colors(), "<b>Figure 9. Heat colors of steel</b> (approximate, viewed in shade). Judge heat in consistent dim light — many smiths shade the forge area for this reason."))
    h.append('<h2 id="ops">4.8 Basic forging operations</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Operation", "What it does", "How"], [
        ["<b>Drawing out</b>", "Makes stock longer and thinner; tapers points", "Heat to orange-yellow. Hammer on the anvil face or over the horn; rotate 90° after each few blows to keep it square. To make round: square → octagon → round. Hammer near the far edge of the anvil for straighter work"],
        ["<b>Upsetting</b>", "Makes stock thicker and shorter (bolt heads, nail heads, hammer faces)", "Heat ONLY the part to be thickened (quench the rest), stand the bar on the anvil and strike the end squarely. Straighten any bend immediately before it worsens"],
        ["<b>Bending</b>", "Hooks, scrolls, rings, angles", "Mark the bend point with a center punch. Bend over the horn, the anvil edge, or in the vise; for a sharp corner, upset first so the outside does not thin"],
        ["<b>Punching</b>", "Holes for rivets, nails, handles (hammer eyes)", "Bright yellow heat. Drive a tapered punch ¾ of the way through, flip the bar — a dark spot shows where to finish — and punch out over the pritchel hole or a hole in a steel block. Cool the punch often; a pinch of coal dust in the hole stops it sticking. Drift the hole to final size"],
        ["<b>Cutting</b>", "Cuts hot stock", "Hot cut chisel or hardy (in the anvil hardy hole): cut most of the way from both sides, then snap. Do not cut through onto the anvil face"],
        ["<b>Twisting</b>", "Decoration, stiffening square bar", "Even heat over the length to twist; grip with tongs or a wrench and turn; quench ends to limit the twist"],
        ["<b>Fullering</b>", "Grooves, necks, shoulders", "Strike with a cross-peen or over a round bar (fuller) to push metal one way"]]))
    h.append(caution("Rules that prevent ruined work", "Never hammer steel below a dull red — it cracks (high carbon) or work-hardens. Do not soak high-carbon steel in a very hot fire — the grain grows coarse and the edge will be weak. Keep the fire deep: work held in the oxidizing zone near the air blast scales heavily. Hit with the flat of the hammer face: edge blows leave “half-moons” that can become cracks."))
    h.append('<h2 id="weld">4.9 Forge welding</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Forge welding joins two pieces of iron or steel by heating both to near-white heat and hammering them together. It was the only way to join metal for thousands of years and is still the way to put a high-carbon steel bit into a mild-steel axe or make chain.</p>')
    h.append('<ol><li><b>Clean, deep fire:</b> well-coked coal or charcoal, at least 4–6 in (10–15 cm) of glowing fuel over the air blast. A dirty fire with clinker will not weld.</li><li><b>Scarf</b> the ends: upset slightly, then hammer each to a short taper so they overlap with a convex face (the weld closes from the middle outward, pushing out slag).</li><li><b>Flux:</b> heat to bright red, sprinkle with <b>borax</b> (laundry borax works; it can be dehydrated in a pan first), or for wrought iron clean fine sand. Flux melts and keeps air off the surface.</li><li>Return to the fire and bring to <b>welding heat</b>: light yellow to near white, with the flux bubbling like water and the first few sparks just starting. Heat both pieces evenly; turn them.</li><li>Move fast: pull both, tap off scale, lay them together on the anvil and strike <b>lightly first</b> to set the weld, then harder. Reheat to welding heat and work the weld again to consolidate it.</li></ol>')
    h.append(tip("Easiest first weld", "Fold a bar of mild steel back on itself (a “faggot” weld) or close a chain link — the pieces are already held together, so you can concentrate on heat. Wrought iron and mild steel weld most easily; high-carbon steel welds at a lower heat (yellow) and burns if overheated."))
    h.append('<h2 id="ht">4.10 Heat treating: annealing, hardening, quenching, tempering</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Heat treatment only works on steel with enough carbon (about 0.5% or more). The metallurgy: heated past its <b>critical temperature</b> (≈ the non-magnetic point, ~1,400–1,500°F / 760–815°C for common carbon steels) steel changes to a structure called austenite that dissolves carbon; quenching fast traps the carbon in a very hard, brittle structure called <b>martensite</b>; tempering then trades a little hardness for toughness.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Step", "Purpose", "How"], [
        ["<b>Anneal</b>", "Make steel as soft as possible for filing, drilling, shaping", "Heat to just past non-magnetic and cool as slowly as possible: buried in dry wood ash, vermiculite or lime overnight, or left in the dying forge"],
        ["<b>Normalize</b>", "Refine the grain and relieve stress after forging", "Heat to just past non-magnetic and cool in still air. Repeat 2–3 times, slightly cooler each time"],
        ["<b>Harden</b>", "Make the edge hard", "Heat evenly to just past non-magnetic (check with a magnet), about a minute at that heat for thin blades — do not overheat"],
        ["<b>Quench</b>", "Cool fast enough to form martensite", "Plunge edge first, straight down, and move up and down (not side to side, which warps). Oil-hardening steels (springs, 5160, 52100, most saw steel): warm (≈120–130°F / 50°C) vegetable oil such as canola. Water-hardening (old files, W1): water or brine — faster but may crack. Test with a file: it should skate"],
        ["<b>Temper</b>", "Reduce brittleness", "IMMEDIATELY after quenching (a hardened blade can crack sitting overnight). Polish to bare metal, reheat slowly and evenly to the color in Figure 10 — in a kitchen oven, over a hot steel bar, or with residual heat from the tool’s thicker end — and quench or let cool"]]))
    h.append(fig(D.tempering_colors(), "<b>Figure 10. Tempering colors</b> on clean, bright steel, with typical uses (after traditional farm-shop tables and TC 9-524)."))
    h.append(tip("Differential hardening — the farm-shop method", "For chisels and punches: heat the working end 2–3 in back, quench only the tip ½–1 in deep, pull it out, quickly polish one side with a stone or sandpaper, and watch the colors run from the hot shank toward the tip. When the tip reaches the right color (purple for a cold chisel, brown/bronze for a wood chisel or axe), quench the whole tool. Hard edge, tough body."))
    h.append(danger("Quench oil fires", "Use a <b>metal</b> container with a <b>tight metal lid</b> and enough oil (several gallons for blades; deep enough to submerge the whole hot part). Submerge fully and quickly — a part held at the surface ignites the oil. If it flames, drop the lid on and leave it. <b>Never use water</b> on an oil fire. Keep a Class B (or ABC) extinguisher nearby. Never quench in gasoline, diesel or used motor oil (toxic fumes and fire). Stand to the side, wear a face shield, long sleeves and leather gloves."))
    h.append('<h2 id="projects">4.11 Projects, from first to advanced</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Project", "Stock", "Key steps"], [
        ["<b>S-hook</b> (first project)", "¼ in (6 mm) round or square mild steel, 6–8 in", "Draw both ends to a taper; curl the tips into small scrolls over the anvil edge; bend each half into a hook in opposite directions over the horn"],
        ["<b>Nails</b>", "¼ in square mild steel; a nail header (a bar with a tapered hole, made by punching)", "Draw a four-sided point; nick the bar at nail length on the hardy, put the point in the header, snap off; upset the head with 4 blows into a rose head. Building timbers can use pegs instead (Part 6)"],
        ["<b>Tongs</b>", "Two pieces of ½–⅝ in round/square mild steel or rebar", "Forge the bit, then the boss (flattened, offset), then draw out the reins. Punch both bosses, rivet hot with a ⅜ in mild-steel rivet, adjust the jaws to the stock while hot. Make flat-jaw, V-bit and bolt tongs"],
        ["<b>Knife</b>", "Leaf spring, old file (annealed), disc blade", "Forge profile and bevels (thin, not sharp), normalize ×3, grind/file to shape, harden, temper to straw-bronze, sharpen, fit a handle with pins or a wrapped tang"],
        ["<b>Cold chisel / punch</b>", "Coil spring, jackhammer bit, axle", "Forge a taper; normalize; harden the tip; temper the working end to purple, the struck end soft (blue or past). Grind off mushrooming forever after"],
        ["<b>Strap hinges and hasps</b>", "⅛–¼ × 1 in flat bar, ⅜ in rod for pins", "Draw and shape the strap; punch nail holes; scroll the end around a mandrel to form the knuckle; cut knuckles to interleave; insert pin"],
        ["<b>Fire tools, pot hooks, trammels</b>", "⅜–½ in round", "Drawing, scrolling, twisting practice"],
        ["<b>Farm tool repair</b>", "As needed", "Re-point a pick or mattock: heat, draw the worn point out, reharden and temper bronze. Sharpen plow shares and cultivator sweeps by drawing out the worn edge (“laying out”), then harden. Weld a broken tine or hoe socket by forge welding with flux"],
        ["<b>Axe repair</b>", "Leaf spring or file steel", "Grind out chips; redress a mushroomed poll on the grinder; re-temper a soft bit (harden ~1½ in of the bit, temper to bronze/purple). Advanced: forge-weld a new high-carbon steel bit into a split worn head"]]))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part5(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p5"><small>Part 5</small>Metalworking Without a Forge &amp; Salvage Welding</h1>']
    h.append('<p>A great deal of useful metalwork needs no fire at all. Mild steel up to about ⅜ in (10 mm) thick, sheet metal, copper, brass and aluminum can be cut, bent, drilled, riveted and threaded cold, using the hand tools from Part 1.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="cold">5.1 Cold working</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Task", "Tools", "Tips"], [
        ["Cutting", "Hacksaw, cold chisel on a steel block, tin snips, bolt cutters, files", "Hacksaw: blade teeth pointing away, at least 3 teeth on the work (fine blades for thin stock), steady full-length strokes ~ 1 per second. Chisel: score both sides, snap in a vise"],
        ["Bending", "Bench vise, pipe as a lever, wooden bending forms", "Mild steel bends cold up to ~ ½ in (12 mm) bar. Bend slowly; mild steel springs back a little. Sheet: bend over a hardwood block with a mallet"],
        ["Drilling", "Breast drill, brace with a metal-drilling bit, hand drill", "Center-punch first; drill a small pilot hole; use cutting oil (any oil, lard) and slow speed with firm pressure"],
        ["Joining", "Rivets (cold set), bolts, folded seams, soft solder", "Rivet: hole slightly larger than rivet, cut rivet length = grip + 1.5 × diameter, set with a ball-peen. Folded seams join sheet without fasteners"],
        ["Threading", "Tap and die set, tap wrench", "Drill the tap-drill size; cut half a turn forward, quarter turn back to break chips; lots of oil. Stockpile common taps (¼-20, 5/16-18, ⅜-16, ½-13, M6, M8, M10)"],
        ["Soldering", "Soldering copper (iron) heated in a fire, flux, solder", "Clean bright metal, flux, heat the WORK until the solder flows. Use lead-free solder for anything touching food or drinking water"],
        ["Annealing copper/brass", "Any fire", "Heat to a dull red and quench in water (opposite of steel) — it becomes soft enough to flare, bend or hammer into gaskets and rivets"],
        ["Peening and dressing", "Ball-peen hammer, anvil or steel block", "Scythe edges, sheet-metal shaping, closing rivets, straightening bent shafts"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="welding">5.2 Salvage welding notes (stick welding)</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Shielded metal arc (stick) welding</b> is the most practical electric welding in a grid-down setting: the equipment is simple and rods store for years if kept dry. It joins and repairs farm equipment, trailers, gates and structures much faster than forge welding. Learn it now in a community college or farm course; the U.S. Army welding manual (TC 9-237) is a thorough reference.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Power source", "What works", "Cautions"], [
        ["Generator + inverter welder", "Small 120 V inverter stick welders (≈ 90–140 A) run on generators of about 3,500–5,000 W; larger 240 V machines need 7 kW+", "Fuel is precious: weld in planned batches. Inverter welders prefer clean power (inverter generators)"],
        ["Engine-drive welder/generator", "Farm and pipeline welders (Lincoln, Miller types) are both welder and generator", "Excellent salvage find; maintain like any engine"],
        ["Battery bank (emergency only)", "Two or three 12 V lead-acid batteries in series (24–36 V) can run 3/32–1/8 in rods for short repairs; current is not regulated (it depends on battery charge, cable length and arc length)", "Very high current: heavy cable, solid clamps. Batteries vent explosive hydrogen and can burst — only for brief emergency repairs, eye/face protection, never near flame or sparks at the battery. It also shortens battery life (see Electricity volume)"]]))
    h.append(table(["Electrode", "Use"], [
        ["E6011", "Deep penetration, works on rusty or dirty steel and with AC or DC — the best general farm-repair rod"],
        ["E6013", "Easy to strike, smooth, shallow — sheet metal and light work, low-power machines"],
        ["E7018", "Strong, low-hydrogen structural welds; must be stored dry (sealed container, warm) or welds crack"]]))
    h.append(danger("Welding hazards", "Arc light burns eyes and skin in seconds (shade 10–13 filter, no exceptions; protect bystanders with screens). Wear dry leather gloves and cotton or wool (synthetics melt). <b>Never weld galvanized or zinc-plated steel, or anything painted with unknown coatings</b>, without removing the coating — zinc fume fever. Never weld or cut a drum or tank that held fuel or chemicals — it can explode even when “empty.” Keep a fire watch for 30 minutes after hot work."))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part6(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p6"><small>Part 6</small>Woodworking for Construction</h1>']
    h.append('<p>With axes, saws, augers and chisels, you can build houses, barns, fences and furniture without nails, screws or electricity — the way most North American buildings were built before about 1850. The House Building volume covers design; this section covers the joints and the green-woodworking methods.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="joinery">6.1 Joinery</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.joinery(), "<b>Figure 11. Basic joints.</b> The pegged mortise and tenon is the heart of timber framing; draw-boring pulls the joint tight without clamps."))
    h.append('<p><b>Cutting a mortise:</b> bore a row of overlapping holes with an auger the width of the mortise, then pare the walls square with a chisel and mallet. <b>Tenon:</b> saw the shoulders across the grain, then split or rip the cheeks. Make the tenon about ⅓ the thickness of the beam and fit it snug but not forced. <b>Draw-boring:</b> drill the peg hole through the mortise walls, insert the tenon and mark the hole, pull it out and drill the tenon hole about ⅛ in (3 mm) closer to the shoulder. A tapered peg driven through pulls the shoulder tight and locks the joint for centuries.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="green">6.2 Green woodworking</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Freshly cut (green) wood is far easier to split, shave and carve than dry wood. The method: <b>split (rive) rather than saw</b> — split wood follows the grain, so it is stronger and does not break across short grain. Use wedges and a maul for logs, a <b>froe</b> and wooden club for boards, shingles (shakes) and handle blanks, and a <b>riving brake</b> (a forked log) to steer the split. Then shape on a shaving horse with drawknife and spokeshave, or hew with a broad axe and adze for beams.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Wood", "Splits well for", "Notes"], [
        ["White and red oak", "Timbers, pegs, clapboards, shingles, baskets", "White oak is rot-resistant and watertight (barrels)"],
        ["Ash", "Handles, chair parts, baskets (pounded splints)", "Emerald ash borer has killed most ash in the East — salvage standing dead ash while sound"],
        ["Hickory", "Handles, pegs, bows, chair rungs", "Toughest; insects attack the sapwood — store dry, bark off"],
        ["Eastern white cedar, western red cedar", "Shingles, posts, fence rails, boat planks", "Rot-resistant, light, splits beautifully"],
        ["Chestnut, black locust, Osage orange", "Fence posts, sills, pegs", "Very rot-resistant; locust posts last decades in the ground"],
        ["White pine, spruce, fir, poplar", "Boards, framing, logs for log houses", "Easy to work, not rot-resistant — keep dry and off the ground"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="pegs">6.3 Making pegs (trunnels)</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Pegs (treenails, “trunnels”) replace nails in timber frames, furniture and boats. Split them from straight-grained, <b>dry</b> oak, locust, hickory or ash — never use sawn pegs, which break along short grain. Rive a billet into squares slightly larger than the hole, shave them to an octagon with a drawknife on the shaving horse, taper the leading end, and let them dry thoroughly. For timber frames use ¾–1 in (19–25 mm) pegs; for furniture ⅜–½ in (10–12 mm). A quick sizing tool: drive pegs through a hole of the right size in a steel plate (a dowel plate) to shave them round. Drive dry pegs into green or seasoned timber; they swell slightly and lock tight.</p>')
    h.append(tip("Drying wood", "Air-dry boards about 1 year per inch (25 mm) of thickness, stacked on stickers (spacers) under a roof with good airflow; seal the ends to stop checking (end cracks). Small parts like pegs and handle blanks dry faster near (not on) a stove. Combine dry tenons with green mortises: the mortise shrinks around the tenon and grips it — the trick behind chairs that last 200 years."))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part7(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p7"><small>Part 7</small>Safety in the Shop and at the Forge</h1>']
    h.append('<p>In a grid-down world, an injury that would be minor today — a burn, an eye wound, a deep cut — can be disabling or fatal without hospitals or antibiotics. Treat these rules as non-negotiable.</p>')
    h.append(danger("Burns", "Most forge burns come from picking up steel that looks cold — steel can be far above 400°F (200°C) with no glow. Rule: <b>treat every piece of metal in a forge shop as hot</b>; mark hot pieces, set them in one place, cool them in the slack tub before setting down. First aid (American Red Cross): stop the burning, cool the burn with cool (not ice-cold) clean running water for at least 10 minutes, remove rings and tight items, cover loosely with a sterile, non-stick dressing. Do not use butter, oil or ice; do not break blisters. Burns that are large, deep (white, leathery or charred), or on the face, hands, feet, groin or joints are serious — get the best medical help available."))
    h.append(danger("Eye protection", "Wear impact-rated safety glasses (ANSI Z87.1 or CSA Z94.3) at the anvil, grinder, when chiseling, knapping, filing and striking hardened steel — hardened tool on hardened tool throws chips like shrapnel. Never strike two hammer faces together. Use a face shield over glasses for grinding and quenching, and proper filter shades for welding and cutting (OSHA eye and face protection guidance). Stockpile spare glasses."))
    h.append(danger("Carbon monoxide (CO)", "Every fire — forge, charcoal kiln, stove, generator, engine — makes CO, an invisible, odorless gas that kills. Early symptoms are headache, dizziness, nausea, weakness and confusion, easily mistaken for flu or tiredness. Forge only outdoors or in an open-sided shed with a hood or chimney; never in a closed garage, basement or tent. Keep battery CO alarms in the shop and home. If anyone feels unwell, get everyone into fresh air immediately."))
    h.append(danger("Galvanized metal: zinc fume fever — never heat galvanized steel", "Galvanized steel (fence posts and wire, pipe, roofing, buckets, garbage cans, trailer frames, many bolts) has a zinc coating. When heated, welded, cut or forged it releases white zinc oxide fumes. Breathing them causes <b>metal fume fever</b>: flu-like chills, fever, metallic taste, cough, nausea and aching starting hours later; heavy or repeated exposure is dangerous. Identify galvanizing by its dull gray, often crystalline “spangle” pattern. Remove it first by soaking in vinegar or muriatic acid outdoors until bubbling stops (with gloves and goggles), or simply leave galvanized material for cold uses. Also avoid heating cadmium-plated (yellowish) parts, brass and bronze (zinc), lead-painted metal, and anything coated with unknown paint or plastic."))
    h.append(table(["Hazard", "Prevention"], [
        ["Fire spread", "Clear 10 ft (3 m) around the forge of dry grass and fuel; no forging on wooden floors; water barrel and ABC extinguisher; douse the fire and check the site before leaving"],
        ["Flying scale and sparks", "Cotton or wool clothes (synthetics melt into skin), leather apron, closed leather boots, trousers over boot tops"],
        ["Noise", "Anvil ring and hammering damage hearing over time — wear ear plugs or muffs; quiet the anvil with a chain or magnet"],
        ["Repetitive strain", "Correct anvil height, a hammer that is not too heavy, relaxed grip; let the hammer rebound. Stop when tired — most accidents happen at the end of the day"],
        ["Grinders", "Wheel guards and tool rest within ⅛ in (3 mm); stand to the side when starting; never use a cracked wheel; no gloves near spinning wheels or drills"],
        ["Cuts from edged tools", "Cut away from your body; keep a “blood circle” (arm + tool length) clear around axes; sheath blades; carry axes head down at your side"],
        ["Silica dust", "Knapping, sandstone grinding and dry sweeping: work outdoors, wet the floor before sweeping, wear a respirator (N95 or better) when possible"],
        ["Hydrogen from batteries", "Charge and weld-from batteries in ventilated areas only, away from sparks and the forge (see Electricity volume)"]]))
    return "\n".join(h)
